#!/usr/bin/env python3
# SPDX-License-Identifier: MPL-2.0
"""cst-quint: model checking of CST's Quint specs with TLC, and the traces their replays read.

A Quint spec (btrfs/tests/quint/*.qnt, vm/tests/quint/*.qnt) models a protocol of a module step by
step, with a crash or a fault after any step. TLC, through quint, explores every state the spec's instances can reach and
checks that each property holds in all of them. A replay test in the unit suite then runs traces of
the spec (checked in, written by --traces) against the real code. See docs/dev-tools.md, "Model
checking with Quint (optional)".

Run it through the invowk task:

    invowk cmd test quint [--traces] [--instance NAME] [--spec FILE] [--keep]

or by hand, from the checkout:

    nice -n 10 /usr/bin/python3 -I -B tests/quint/cst-quint [options]

Options:
    --instance NAME   only this instance (a module of a spec, such as nocow_ab or nas_live1)
    --spec FILE       check FILE instead of the checked-in spec (a planted copy, say); with checks of
                      several specs, only with --instance
    --traces          write the checked-in traces again (simulator and Apalache), then run the replay
    --keep            keep the work folder (it names it at the end)

The tools are pinned and fetched into ${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-~/.cache}/cst-quint (a relative
XDG_CACHE_HOME is ignored: tests/xdg_cache.py), never into the checkout: quint (npm ci --ignore-scripts from tests/quint/package-lock.json, which pins every package
with its hash), and the two programs quint would otherwise download unchecked on first use,
Apalache (whose jar also runs TLC) and the Rust evaluator, each checked against its SHA-256 before it
is unpacked into QUINT_HOME. A tool is used as it is only when the stamp beside it records the
hash that was checked. quint, Apalache and TLC run from a work folder under the same cache (they
write _apalache-out/ in the current folder), under nice, each with a time limit, TLC with 4 GB of
heap and 4 workers (quint's defaults are 8 GB and every CPU). Each quint verify gets its own localhost
port, free on IPv4 and IPv6 when it starts and outside the kernel's ephemeral range (so no outgoing connection
is given it before Apalache binds it), for the Apalache server quint starts and stops with it, so two runs at
once neither share a server nor collide on quint's default port 8822.

It needs node and npm, java (17 or later) and curl. Exit status: 0 every property holds (and with
--traces, the replay passes); 1 a property is violated, a run timed out or failed, or a download did
not match; 2 a usage error or a missing tool.
"""

